/* THE TEN STAMPS — RECONSTRUCTED, NOT DESIGNED.

   The stamp card carries the ten Sacred Treasures of Isonokami: the two
   mirrors, the sword, the four jewels and the three scarves. Slot N of
   the card holds treasure N, repeating every tenth stamp, so a full card
   is the whole set rather than one mark ten times.

   These paths are a vector reconstruction of a supplied reference
   drawing, not an interpretation of it. Each symbol's ink/paper boundary
   was traced from the reference bitmap at sub-pixel accuracy — every
   outer edge and every interior cutout — and the result agrees with the
   source at a mean intersection-over-union of 0.997 (worst 0.989).

   Nothing about the artwork is normalised between symbols. They do not
   share a weight, a density, a stroke, a frame or a silhouette, because
   the reference does not: `taru` and `chika` are wide and sparse, the
   scarves are dense, `oki` is tall and thin, and the two teardrops carry
   fringes of hairlines. The ONE thing made uniform is outer display
   size — each symbol is scaled so its longest side is 58 of the 64-unit
   box and centred there, which is what lets ten differently-shaped marks
   sit in one grid of square slots. Every symbol keeps its own aspect
   ratio inside that box, so a wide symbol stays wide.

   `fill-rule="evenodd"` is what makes the cutouts work: the contours are
   traced independently and nested, so a ring inside a solid reads as a
   hole with no winding bookkeeping at all.

   Paths, not rects or strokes, deliberately: the stylesheets key colour
   off `path` for artwork and off `circle`/`line` for registration
   furniture, so a <rect> falls through every rule and paints default
   black — invisible the moment a slot inverts to an ink field. For the
   same reason nothing here is stroked; a stroke on a 64-unit box closes
   the fine openings and turns a reconstruction into a blob.

   Regenerate with scratchpad/trace.py against the reference bitmap. */
